\documentclass[12pt]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=2.6cm]{geometry}
\usepackage{fontspec}
\setmainfont{TeX Gyre Pagella}
\newfontfamily\displayfont{TeX Gyre Heros}
\newfontfamily\titlefont{TeX Gyre Adventor}
\newfontfamily\notefont{TeX Gyre Cursor}

\title{\titlefont Aldine House Press\\
Type Specimen Sheet No.~7}
\author{\displayfont The Aldine House Press, Newark}
\date{\displayfont Autumn 2014}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section*{\displayfont The Text Face}
The text face of the press is a garalde of even colour and
generous x-height, chosen for long reads in damp basements
and draughty binderies alike.
Its italic leans a gentle eight degrees and keeps the
swash capitals in check until the final line of a chapter.

\section*{\displayfont Ligatures and Figures}
The standard ligatures fire without ceremony:
ff fi fl ffi ffl ft ct st.
Lining figures suit the ledger, while text figures
{ \addfontfeature{Numbers=OldStyle} 0123456789 }
sit quietly among lowercase letters in a running page.
Small caps carry the running heads:
\textsc{the aldine house press, newark}.

\section*{\displayfont The Display Face}
For title pages and the covers of chapbooks the press keeps
a humanist sans, set tight and tracked a hair wide:

\begin{center}
{\displayfont\Large The Basket of Apples}\\[4pt]
{\displayfont\large a chapbook of orchard songs}\\[4pt]
{\displayfont\small aldine house press --- mcmxiv}
\end{center}

\section*{\displayfont A Sample Page}
\textit{The pressman rises before the bell, washes the
ink from his forearms, and sets the first line of the day
as if the whole edition depended upon it --- which, in a
manner of speaking, it does.}

\section*{\displayfont Colophon}
{\notefont This sheet was composed at the press by the
apprentice of the second shift and pulled on the small
proof press in an edition of forty copies, of which this
is number seven.}

\end{document}
